From f217644070d1e821775f1644563dde54db750ae6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan <jojarra@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 02:42:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Colour the chambers as the building does, keep every
 edition at its own address, and draw the corpus one response at a time
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Chamber colours. The House's eucalyptus and the Senate's ochre, as tokens
(--house, --senate, with dark-mode variants that clear 4.5:1), used only
to say which register a thing belongs to: the rule above a hero tile and
the chamber's name in it, the active item in the nav on a register's own
page, the eyebrow label and the rule above the stat tiles, the bar under
each wait, and a dot beside a register's name wherever a row could
belong to either. Never the colour of a number — an ochre 82 beside a
green 38 would say the Senate is failing and the House is fine, which is
the comparison this site says must not be made — and never colour alone:
every use sits beside the chamber's name, because one man in twelve
cannot tell the two apart.

The clock. Under the longest wait, the same interval the register prints
in days, still running: hours, minutes and seconds since midnight in
Canberra on the day the report was tabled, with the offset worked out at
build time so every reader sees the same count. Decorative and
aria-hidden; stops at minutes for anyone who has asked for less motion.
The days figure advances at midnight, which is the only number on the
site that moves between builds, and it moves because time does.

The strip. One square per response in the classified corpus, in tabling
order — the same 623 and 260 as everywhere else, drawn one document at a
time, with the busiest day picked out in the site's blue. It shows the
one thing the yearly chart cannot: that the closures arrive in runs.

Editions. Every weekly build now writes data/editions/<date>.json after
the publishability gate — both registers' rows, the closure figures, the
checked-to date — and the site publishes it unchanged at /as-at/<date>/,
so a figure quoted from this site stays reachable at the page that
showed it after next week's run has moved it. The commit is tagged
edition-<date> so the dataset behind the page can be found. The footer
names the edition on every page, with the address to cite, and once two
editions exist it says what moved between them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SpXDcq1rRGJpWt2bRHkpcY
